Optimizing Open Client/Server Computing

IS departments planning or implementing an open client/server strategy
typically require powerful hardware, client/server application software, and
comprehensive consulting services to tie together and upgrade their existing
installations. To achieve maximum results, they seek solutions offering high
performance, high availability, and high reliability. Yet many of the
solutions they need are either too costly or don't provide the necessary
features or support to fully implement an open computing environment.

As a follow-on to two earlier announcements of open client/server computing
solutions, Digital has unveiled new Alpha AXP hardware and configurations,
advanced VAX systems, operating system enhancements, new software, and an
expanded applications portfolio. These offerings enable IS departments to
fully implement and optimize their open client/server environments while
delivering the functionality and open standards compliance required for
general purpose, mission critical, or high performance computing.

This article provides an overview of the components of Digital's April 12
announcement. For further details, please refer to the individual articles
throughout this publication.

HARDWARE

Digital's hardware rollout features the founding members of a new scalable
family of affordable symmetric multiprocessing (SMP) servers. The Digital
2100 Server Models A500MP, A500MP-R, and A600MP offer record
price/performance, scalability, expandability, and high availability. With
support for up to four CPUs, they are the first servers in the industry to
incorporate the low cost, high performance Peripheral Component Interconnect
(PCI) bus on a multiprocessing platform.

Based on the Alpha AXP architecture, Digital 2100 Server systems support
three major operating systems: the DEC OSF/1, OpenVMS, and Windows NT
Advanced Server systems. They are available in deskside pedestal,
multisystem CPU cabinet, and rackmount configurations that are covered by a
three-year onsite hardware warranty with a 24-hour or next-business-day
response time.

Digital is selling Digital 2100 Server systems individually or in specially
priced configurations when purchased with major database applications
including INGRES, ORACLE, SYBASE, and Microsoft SQL Server (see the order
numbers at the end of this article). You can order these packages, which are
part of Digital's PowerChannel program, through Digital's DECdirect ordering
system or authorized Digital resellers. These packages will be available
through September 30, 1994.

For high performance computing, Digital has also unveiled AdvantageCluster
servers. Like Digital 2100 Server systems, AdvantageCluster servers are
based on the Alpha AXP architecture, but they employ "workstation farm" or
"cluster" technology for low cost, high performance computing. The new
AdvantageCluster servers include four AdvantageCluster Compute Servers, for
compute intensive applications; the AdvantageCluster File Server, which
provides NFS services to workgroups; and the AdvantageCluster Available
Server, for organizations with high availability needs.

Two new VAX systems deliver more power than earlier models but at the same
prices. The VAX 4000 Model 105A system offers 33 percent more power than the
Model 100A but at the same price. The MicroVAX 3100 Model 95 provides 30
percent more power than the Model 90 system while the price remains the
same. Board upgrades from the older models are available.

DEC OSF/1 SOFTWARE

Highlighting the newest version of the DEC OSF/1 operating system, V3.0, is
support for SMP scaling in Alpha AXP systems, including the new Digital 2100
Server systems. If you have SMP platform requirements, you can upgrade your
current systems easily and economically with inbox upgrades instead of
entire system additions. Other new features in DEC OSF/1 V3.0 include a
loadable drivers option, which permits the addition of device drivers
without rebooting or rebuilding the kernel; dataless support, for low cost,
entry level, easy-to-manage PC LAN support; and standards compliant system
services, for applications with critical timing demands that require fast
and predictable response times.

For high performance computing, Digital now groups all its technical
computing software that runs on the DEC OSF/1 operating system in one
package. TechAdvantage software contains all the necessary elements that you
need to write efficient code for either single stream or parallel
processing. The TechAdvantage package features the new DEC Fortran 90
compiler, which automatically "parallelizes" software to run across systems
in a cluster computing environment, and the Digital Parallel Software
Environment, which enables execution and debugging of parallel DEC Fortran
90 applications on the Alpha AXP platform.

Windows NT AND Windows NT Advanced Server SOFTWARE

Microsoft Corporation, developer of Windows NT and Windows NT Advanced
Server, will begin shipping a new release of the operating system in June.
This release, called Daytona, will offer up to 25 percent improved
performance and a 4 Mbyte reduction in memory. Windows NT and Windows NT
Advanced Server run on the DEC 2000 Model 500 system, the DECpc AXP/150
personal computer, and the new Digital 2100 Server systems.

For Digital 2100 Server systems, the enhanced Windows NT Advanced Server
operating system will support the PCI bus. It will also feature built-in SMP
support, as well as software based RAID that works with optional RAID
hardware for database security and performance.

Also for Windows NT systems, Digital has unveiled the DCE V1.0 for Windows
NT product. This software is the first implementation of the Distributed
Computing Environment available for Windows NT systems.

OpenVMS SOFTWARE

With the release of OpenVMS V6.1, the OpenVMS operating system is now
functionally equivalent on Alpha AXP and VAX systems. In other words,
OpenVMS V6.1 now provides the same user, system management, and programming
environments, security services, system integrated products, and standards
compliance for both Digital platforms.

Digital has also unveiled future plans for major enhancements to the OpenVMS
operating system. These enhancements will feature a log structured file
system that will sharply improve performance and backup while maintaining
clusterwide integrity, breakthroughs in multivendor integration, 64 bit file
and application support, an extension of clustering support for wide-area
disaster-tolerant computing, and XPG4 X/Open branding.

APPLICATIONS

For more than 18 months, Digital has been working with software vendors to
expand the number of open applications running on Alpha AXP systems. As a
result, the total number of software products available on Alpha AXP systems
running the DEC OSF/1, OpenVMS, or Windows NT operating systems will reach
5,800 applications by midyear. These applications include leading business
and technical software, such as ORACLE7 database software (ported to the DEC
OSF/1 and OpenVMS platforms), SAP's R/3 for financial operations (DEC OSF/1,
NT, and OpenVMS platforms), and PowerHouse from Cognos for commercial
software development (DEC OSF/1 and OpenVMS platforms).

Digital's application partners are now shipping 2,300 UNIX applications for
DEC OSF/1 systems, 2,300 applications for OpenVMS systems, and 400
applications for Windows NT systems. By the end of June, Digital expects to
have 5,800 Alpha AXP applications available.

