EtherWORKS Update

IN THIS SECTION:

o  Turbo EISA (DE425) NIC for Windows NT AXP

o  PCMCIA Turbo and Turbo Plus PC NICs

o  Turbo PCI (DE435) 32 Bit Ethernet PC Adapter

EtherWORKS TURBO EISA (DE425) NIC FOR Windows NT AXP

To maximize system and network data throughput for Windows NT AXP systems
with an EISA bus, Digital is now offering its EtherWORKS Turbo EISA (DE425)
network interface card (NIC). This 32 bit, bus master, high performance
Ethernet PC controller is designed for full duplex operation providing 20
Mbps connections when transmit and receive are activated simultaneously. It
is a DMA device with two 256 byte-on-chip FIFOs; it has a capture effect
avoidance algorithm for low latency and network fairness for all nodes.

When you use the EtherWORKS Turbo EISA (DE425) NIC on an Alpha AXP server
running Windows NT, you have the benefit of the highest system CPU time
available for applications while clients are serviced at the maximum
Ethernet bandwidth.

If you want to use a 20 Mbit line rate, you can do so by installing an
EtherWORKS Turbo EISA (DE425) NIC on a client that is connected to a server
with the same card, and then setting both PC NICs to full duplex mode.

The EtherWORKS Turbo EISA (DE425) NIC is available now.

Order No.	 Description

DE425-AA	 EtherWORKS Turbo EISA (DE425) NIC

EtherWORKS PCMCIA TURBO AND TURBO PLUS PC NICs

Until recently, docking stations or external network adapters have been the
only means of connecting portable and notebook personal computers to a local
area network. Both have had drawbacks based on cost, size, or data transfer
rates.

Digital's EtherWORKS PCMCIA Turbo and Turbo Plus PC NICs provide a cost
effective way to connect portable and notebook personal computers directly
to a LAN. These Ethernet adapter cards have a PCMCIA form factor suitable
for any personal computers with a PCMCIA Type II slot.

If you need connections for twisted pair cabling, you use the EtherWORKS
Turbo NIC; if you need connections for ThinWire and/or twisted pair cabling,
you use the EtherWORKS Turbo Plus NIC.

By using either adapter, you eliminate the need for a docking station. This
saves equipment costs as well as space on the desktop. If you need data
rates higher than those provided by external adapters, note that the
EtherWORKS PCMCIA Turbo or Turbo Plus NICs transfer data on IEEE 802.3
compliant networks at rates up to 10 Mbits per second.

Software drivers for connecting the PATHWORKS, NetWare, LAN Manager, Windows
for Workgroups, and VINES (client) network operating systems are included
with the adapter. Once the drivers are loaded into the system, the adapters
can be hot-swapped without reloading the drivers.

The EtherWORKS PCMCIA Turbo and Turbo Plus PC NICs will be available next
month (May 1994).

Order No.	 Description

DEPCM-AA	 EtherWORKS PCMCIA Turbo NIC with twisted pair cable
DEPCM-BA	 EtherWORKS PCMCIA Turbo Plus NIC with twisted pair and BNC
                 cable

EtherWORKS TURBO PCI (DE435) 32 BIT ETHERNET PC ADAPTER

The new, industry standard PCI (peripheral component interconnect) bus is a
high performance bus with a maximum data transfer rate of 132 Mbytes per
second -- more than four times the speed of the EISA bus. Alpha AXP and
Intel systems (including DECpc XL systems) with the PCI bus in network
environments need a high performance Ethernet adapter, and Digital's new
EtherWORKS Turbo PCI adapter fills this need.

This high performance, 32 bit Ethernet PC adapter allows high data transfer
rates for optimum network performance with very low system CPU utilization.
It has a DMA bus master design with a single, CMOS, full custom, VLSI
Ethernet chip designed by Digital. Its unique, patent pending intelligent
bus arbitration algorithm ensures that all nodes on small networks will have
equal access to it.

The adapter supports full duplex mode when a server and client are set up
for it. Full duplex mode provides a data transfer rate of 20 Mbits per
second. The adapter is easy to install and in addition it is
auto-configurable while supporting three cable interfaces: ThinWire, twisted
pair, and thick wire. Its software drivers connect to the PATHWORKS,
NetWare, LAN Manager, Windows NT, SCO UNIX, OS/2, and VINES (client) network
operating systems. The adapter is available now.

Order No.	 Description

