Distributed Computing Environment (DCE) for Windows NT

HIGHLIGHTS

o  First DCE product in the industry for Windows NT platforms lets you
   develop applications that interoperate between PC and workstation
   platforms

o  Provides simple, powerful computing environment for building, using, and
   maintaining client/server applications

o  Improves time to market by providing tools that ease development of
   client/server applications

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

Many users have already adopted the Open Software Foundation Distributed
Computing Environment (OSF DCE) as their infrastructure for distributed
computing. The OSF DCE provides an industry standard set of services that
enable distributed applications to be developed easily and interoperate
across heterogeneous platforms transparently. The DCE supplies a common
services infrastructure (naming and security) that greatly simplifies
application development for mixed vendor environments. Digital has now
extended this environment to the Windows NT platform.

DCE V1.0 for Windows NT provides a full, interoperable set of the
distributed computing functionality specified by the OSF DCE. This includes:
the DCE Remote Procedure Call (RPC), which creates and runs client/server
applications (on Windows NT platforms, the RPC is actually provided as part
of the Windows NT operating system); the DCE Cell Directory Service (CDS),
which provides location independent naming for resources; the DCE
Distributed Time Service (DTS), which synchronizes time in a distributed
network environment; the DCE Security Service, which provides secure
communications and access via authorization and authentication services; and
the DCE Threads Service, which provides user context multiprocessing
functionality. These versions of DCE from Digital are based on OSF DCE
V1.0.2 and use Windows NT 3.1.

DCE V1.0 for Windows NT requires the Daytona release of Windows NT from
Microsoft as a prerequisite. This release provides the Microsoft RPC that is
compliant with the OSF DCE RPC specification.

In addition to the base capabilities specified by the OSF, DCE V1.0 for
Windows NT includes these enhancements of the standard DCE offering,
providing a number of benefits:

o  A transparent mapping of the OSF RPC API to the Microsoft RPC API. This
   allows DCE applications to be transparently ported to the Windows NT
   platform.

o  Transparent use of either the DCE services or the native Windows NT
   services, through the use of the Microsoft Windows NT RPC.

o  The ability to distribute Windows NT applications over an enterprise
   network, by using the DCE services. The DCE on Windows NT product allows
   Windows NT applications to achieve enterprise connectivity, which cannot
   be achieved without the DCE services.

o  The X/Open Directory Services (XDS) programming interface. This allows
   applications that use this interface to find a name in the CDS or an
   X.500 directory transparently.

o  To ease system management, simplified installation and configuration as
   well as a namespace browser.

o  A comprehensive set of consulting and integration services to ease the
   migration to a client/server environment.

The Digital DCE V1.0 for Windows NT product family consists of four
products:

o  The Digital DCE Runtime Services kit -- required for all systems
   participating in the DCE cell. The kit includes DCE administration tools
   as well as DCE client functions.

o  The Digital DCE Application Developer's kit -- required for developers of
   distributed applications. This kit provides programmers with an Interface
   Definition Language (IDL), which is an easy-to-use, ANSI C based language
   for writing remote procedure calls.

o  A Digital DCE Cell Directory Server (CDS) -- one of which is required for
   each DCE cell. The server is a central repository containing information
   about the location of resources in the DCE cell. It allows access to
   resources by a single name, regardless of physical location.

o  A Digital DCE Security Server -- one of which is required for each DCE
   cell. This server protects resources from illegal access and provides
   secure communications within and between DCE cells.

The DCE runtime services on Windows NT can use any OSF DCE cell directory or
security server provided by another vendor, regardless of platform.

BENEFITS

o  For the application programmer:
   - A consistent, standardized, easy-to-use programming environment for
     building distributed applications.
   - A convenient mechanism to integrate Windows NT platforms into the
     distributed environment. It allows application interoperability between
     Windows NT and other platforms and easy portability of applications
     between platforms.

o  For the end user:
   - Faster, easier access to data, systems, and other resources from
     Windows NT systems.

o  For the system manager:
   - Installation, configuration, and management of the distributed network
     are simpler than in other DCE implementations.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

The DCE for Windows NT Runtime Services kit and the DCE Application
Developer's kit will be available in June 1994. The DCE Cell Directory
Server and DCE Security Server will be available in September 1994. Until
the Windows NT servers are available, any OSF compliant DCE server from
either Digital (on the DEC OSF/1 AXP platform) or another vendor can be
used.

Digital's DCE products for Windows NT will be packaged with the PATHWORKS
product set in addition to being licensed standalone.

