FUNCTIONAL EQUIVALENCE

The OpenVMS AXP operating system is now functionally equivalent to the
OpenVMS VAX operating system, and both carry the V6.1 designation to
acknowledge this parity. The OpenVMS AXP V6.1 release directly follows the
OpenVMS AXP V1.0 and V1.5 releases.

Functional equivalence means that the OpenVMS AXP operating system will
provide you with the same user environment, security services, system
management environment, programmer environment, standards compliance, and
system integrated products that you have valued in the OpenVMS VAX operating
system, though the software implementation or user interface may vary
slightly.

It also offers the same total data protection, reliability, and
availability provided by OpenVMS VAX software. OpenVMS AXP V6.1 lets you
build on your investment in VAX systems while you take advantage of the
computing power and price/performance leadership of the most advanced RISC
technology available, that of the Alpha AXP platform. It provides the
fastest running implementation of OpenVMS software ever achieved.

The Alpha AXP platform also provides the full software environment required
to support mission critical applications. A complete suite of database
products, PC connectivity tools, and transaction processing and network
software will be available on the OpenVMS AXP platform. Digital's popular
ALL-IN-1 office automation software will be available in the third quarter
this year.

Many users have already expanded their OpenVMS cluster environments by
adding Alpha AXP systems, allowing them to continue sharing resources across
systems as well as across architectures. Only OpenVMS software offers this
premier clustering capability -- on both VAX and Alpha AXP processors. With
OpenVMS AXP V6.1, cross-architectural booting of satellites is now possible.

FUNCTIONALITY OVERVIEW

OpenVMS AXP V6.1 includes all the features provided in OpenVMS AXP V1.5,
plus the following new installation, cluster, and security features:

o  The POLYCENTER Software Installation Utility is available for installing
   the OpenVMS AXP operating system and layered products. This new
   technology simplifies the distribution and management of software. Its
   menu driven procedure allows you to upgrade or install the operating
   system easily. It also provides system managers with Digital Command
   Language (DCL) and DECwindows Motif interfaces that can be used to
   configure, install, and remove software products that are PCSI compliant.

o  The security features in OpenVMS VAX V6.0, which have achieved the U.S.
   Department of Defense's National Computer Security Center (NCSC) C2
   security rating, are now available on OpenVMS AXP systems.

o  VMScluster functionality has been enhanced in OpenVMS AXP V6.1 as
   follows:

   - Virtual I/O cache is now supported in VMScluster systems when the VAX
     systems in the cluster are running OpenVMS VAX V6.0 or later. Virtual
     I/O cache is a clusterwide, file oriented disk cache that reduces I/O
     bottlenecks and improves performance.

   - Clusterwide tape serving is now supported by OpenVMS AXP systems. The
     OpenVMS tape mass storage control protocol allows locally connected
     tape drives to be accessible to all members of a VMScluster
     configuration.

   - Cross-architecture satellite booting is available in a VMScluster
     system running OpenVMS AXP and VAX V6.1. This new feature allows VAX
     boot nodes to provide boot service to AXP satellites, and AXP boot
     nodes to provide boot service to VAX satellites.

   - DEC Availability Manager for Distributed Systems (DECamds) is a
     realtime monitoring, investigation, diagnostic, and software correction
     tool to assist system managers and system integrators in improving
     system availability on OpenVMS systems. DECamds software is now
     included with the base OpenVMS operating system as a separately
     installable product and is enabled with the VMScluster software
     license.

o  The enhanced device driver interface (known as the Step 2 interface) and
   user written device drivers are now supported. The Step 2 interface
   provides support for writing device drivers in the C programming language
   or another high level language that can conform to the OpenVMS AXP
   calling standard.

OpenVMS AXP V6.1 also includes the following new features and hardware
support:

o  Movefile support.
o  File system performance enhancements.
o  DECdtm V1.2.
o  Multiple queue manager support.
o  Modifiable LAT symbiont.
o  Native MACRO-32 compiler.
o  Reintegration of volume shadowing.
o  New hardware support:
   - Digital 2100 Server Model A500MP hardware support.
   - KFMSB support for DEC 7000 AXP and DEC 10000 AXP systems.
o  Reintegration of OpenVMS AXP V1.5-1H1 hardware support:
   - DEC 2000 Model 300 AXP.
   - DEC 3000 Model 600 and Model 600S AXP.
   - DEC 3000 Model 800 and Model 800S AXP.
   - PMAZC TURBOchannel adapter.

Though the OpenVMS AXP and VAX versions do provide functionally equivalent
capabilities, there are a few differences that will be addressed in the next
version of OpenVMS AXP software. OpenVMS AXP V6.1 does not include the
following features, which are provided in OpenVMS VAX V6.1:

o  DECnet/OSI Fullnames support.
o  SCSI-2 Tagged Command Queuing.

For more product information, please consult OpenVMS AXP Software Product
Description 41.87. For more information on OpenVMS AXP and VAX functional
equivalence, see the "Functional Equivalence of OpenVMS VAX and AXP with
V6.1" article in the February 14, 1994, issue of Digital's Customer Update.
For information on VMScluster software and mixed architecture cluster
support, see VMScluster SPD 42.18 and the cluster software article in this
issue.

LAYERED PRODUCT AVAILABILITY

Shipping at the same time as OpenVMS AXP V6.1 will be the second quarter
Alpha AXP layered products software library. This library includes the
versions of Digital layered products that are currently supported for
OpenVMS AXP V6.1. This is a cumulative list of products that have shipped
since the introduction of OpenVMS AXP software in November 1992. These
products include Digital's database, network, and transaction processing
software and PC connectivity tools.

INVESTMENT PROTECTION

Your OpenVMS VAX investments are completely protected with the introduction
of OpenVMS AXP V6.1. More than 80 percent of your total investment in an
OpenVMS VAX environment is in personnel, training, applications, data,
networks, clusters, and peripherals -- but only the CPU changes when
upgrading between the two architectures. The full set of Digital's OpenVMS
AXP compilers will be available, so you can easily move your existing
applications to the Alpha AXP platform and take advantage of its faster
processing power. If the application's sources are not available, Digital's
"DECmigrate for OpenVMS AXP Systems" tool will translate OpenVMS VAX
executable images into OpenVMS AXP executables.

The OpenVMS AXP and VAX operating systems will continue to be enhanced in
the future. Digital's goal is to maintain functional equivalence for future
versions of the OpenVMS operating systems. One of the main areas of emphasis
will be the support of mixed architecture clusters (VMScluster
configurations consisting of OpenVMS AXP and VAX systems). As you take
advantage of the architectural richness of Alpha AXP systems (as in 64 bit
virtual addressing), Digital will continue to protect your investments by
maintaining coexistence in the mixed architecture cluster environment.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

OpenVMS AXP V6.1 will begin shipping in May 1994. Media and documentation
are separately orderable. OpenVMS AXP binaries are distributed on a compact
disc that also contains OpenVMS AXP documentation in online Bookreader
format. Online documentation can be accessed with the Bookreader function of
the DECwindows Motif for OpenVMS AXP layered product (SPD 42.19). Hardcopy
documentation is also available and is ordered separately.

Systems that ship with factory installed software will be delivered with
OpenVMS AXP V6.1 preinstalled.

