StorageWorks RAID Array 210 Subsystem

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Hardware based RAID subsystem available as internal option for Digital
   2100 Server and as standalone RAID subsystem in combination with
   StorageWorks disk drives and enclosures

o  EISA backplane RAID controller provides RAID levels 0 (striping), 1
   (shadowing), 0+1 (striped shadowing), and 5 (striping with parity)

o  Supports up to 21 disk drives per controller, two controllers per system

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

As applications move from mainframes to client/server environments, the need
for high availability storage solutions supporting higher capacities extends
beyond the datacenter and into networked environments. The StorageWorks RAID
Array 210 subsystem provides high capacity, low cost storage solutions with
high availability and improved I/O performance. RAID (Redundant Array of
Independent Disks) is a way of configuring disk drives to provide increased
performance and/or data redundancy in the event of a drive failure.

The heart of the StorageWorks RAID Array 210 subsystem is the SWXCR-Ex EISA
backplane RAID controller. The SWXCR-Ex is available in two variants that
will support either one or three SCSI buses with up to seven disk drives per
bus. Other key features of the subsystem:

o  System bus interface: industry standard EISA
o  Controller form factor: industry standard EISA card
o  RAID levels supported: 0 (striping, no redundancy), 1 (mirroring or
   shadowing), 0+1 (striping with  mirroring), 5 (striping with distributed
   parity), JBOD (disk drive)
o  Drive channels supported: 1 or 3 SCSI channels
o  SCSI channels: SCSI-2, 8 bit, single-ended, fast (10 Mbytes per second)
o  Number of disks supported: up to 7 per channel (SWXCR-EA = 7 disk drives,
   SWXCR-EB = 21 disk drives)
o  Non-RAID device support: disk drive
o  Drives supported: RZ25L-VA (0.5 Gbytes), RZ26L-VA (1.05 Gbytes), RZ28-VA
   (2.1 Gbytes)
o  Automatic reconstruct
o  Disk hot swap
o  Disk hot spare
o  Redundant power supplies
o  Redundant fans
o  Configurable reconstruct time
o  Systems supported: Alpha AXP platforms with EISA bus
o  Number of controllers per system: 2
o  Cache support: 4 to 32 Mbytes (4 Mbytes standard)
o  Mixed RAID levels
o  Mixed drive types
o  Operating system support: OpenVMS AXP, DEC OSF/1 AXP, Windows NT AXP

BENEFITS

You can configure your disk drives to maximize performance, to safeguard
data against the failure of a disk drive, or both.

CONFIGURING A StorageWorks RAID ARRAY 210 SUBSYSTEM

A StorageWorks RAID Array 210 subsystem can be configured with the disk
drives inside the system enclosure or in standalone configurations, where
the disk devices are contained in StorageWorks pedestal enclosures. In
either case, the subsystem is configured by a simple "sum-of-the-pieces"
scheme that uses standard StorageWorks components (pedestals, power
supplies, disk drives, fans, and so on). This vastly reduces the number of
orderable parts and provides growth and configuration flexibility.

A subsystem consists of the following key components, which are all ordered
separately:

o  An SWXCR-Ex EISA backplane RAID controller, which plugs  directly into
   the EISA backplane of the target system.

o  A software and documentation kit, which includes user documentation and
   appropriate device drivers and RAID configuration software for the system
   platform. You order a software and documentation kit any time you order
   an SWXCR-Ex controller.

o  StorageWorks disk drives (RZ25L-VA, RZ26L-VA, RZ28-VA).

o  StorageWorks enclosures (BA350-KA), if needed, to house the disks.

o  Cables to connect the controller to the disk drives.

o  Additional StorageWorks components (redundant power supplies, fans,
   and other components).

The following configurations are offered as examples of how to configure a
StorageWorks RAID Array 210 subsystem. For the purpose of these examples it
is assumed that the RZ26L-VA 1.05 Gbyte disk drive is used and that the
subsystem is a standalone configuration (that is, the disk drives are
external to the system enclosure) for a Windows NT AXP system. These basic
configurations can be modified to fit most applications.

