Windows NT and Windows NT Advanced Server Daytona Release

HIGHLIGHTS

o  New release will provide performance improvements in terms of speed and
   memory requirements

o  New release will support several new features, including OpenGL, NWCS,
   OLE2, and faster TCP/IP and IPX/SPX stacks

o  Windows NT Advanced Server beta release will support Digital 2100 Server
   family

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

The Daytona release of Windows NT and Windows NT Advanced Server will
include the following enhancements:

o  Significant performance improvements accompanied by reduced memory
   requirements to support the operating system: You will see performance
   improvements of up to 25 percent across the board, while improvements in
   the working set will mean that up to 4 Mbytes less memory will be needed
   for the same performance level.

o  Windows 16 applications will run more reliably.

o  You will be able to connect to NetWare servers out of the box. NetWare
   Workstation Compatible Software (NWCS) will give Windows NT clients
   access to NetWare file, print, and application servers.

o  The next generation of object-oriented applications will be supported,
   and native OLE2 will be supported with full 16-bit-to-32-bit application
   integration.

o  Industry standard OpenGL support for graphic applications will be added.

o  New and faster TCP/IP and IPX/SPX stacks with automatic addressing and
   configuration will be included.

New features available only in Windows NT Advanced Server will include
account lockout and system dump facilities for easy troubleshooting, in
addition to easy dial-in to NetWare and UNIX servers. SMP will also be
supported for the Alpha AXP platforms with the availability of the Digital
2100 Server family. (Please note that only Windows NT Advanced Server will
support the Digital 2100 Servers.)

BENEFITS

o  The new releases enhance an operating system that already addresses the
   major elements you need to consider in moving to client/server computing.

o  The combination of Digital and Microsoft products provides you with a
   broad family of high performance systems. Each company brings its area of
   expertise to the evolving client/server technology, and each has the goal
   of enhancing workgroup and departmental productivity with Windows NT and
   Windows NT Advanced Server in a way that is analogous to the personal
   productivity gains that came from Windows.

o  The combination of Windows NT Advanced Server and the Digital 2100 Server
   family offers you outstanding performance in managing a large number of
   PC clients or running distributed database applications. Windows NT
   Advanced Server is ideal for the new Digital 2100 Servers because it
   supports PCI, has SMP support built in, and features software based RAID
   that works with optional RAID hardware to provide outstanding database
   security and performance.

o  Applications vendors are increasingly bringing their products to Windows
   NT, especially to Alpha AXP based Windows NT. Nearly 400 applications run
   now on Alpha AXP based Windows NT systems and over 1,000 product ports
   will be shipping this summer. Included are Microsoft's SQL Server and
   Office, INFORMIX, and many more.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

The Daytona release of Windows NT and Windows NT Advanced Server will be
available late in the second quarter this year. (The beta test version of
Windows NT Advanced Server will be included in the Early Ship Program for
Digital 2100 Server Models A500MP and A600MP. For details, see the
accompanying article in this issue.)

With these new releases, Digital will continue to offer Windows NT products
in packaged hardware systems and as the operating system component in user
specified configurations. No order numbers will change.

Digital plans to make Windows NT and Windows NT Advanced Server upgrade kits
available at product release. These kits will be offered to users of Digital
Windows NT systems (both Intel and Alpha AXP based) who are not covered
under a Digital software update contract.

Note that licensing for Windows NT and Windows NT Advanced Server is on a
per-version basis. The new releases will therefore require a new license for
newly purchased systems or an upgrade license for existing systems.

For ordering and other product information, please consult the Windows NT
articles in the May 10, 1993, May 31, 1993, August 30, 1993, September 27,
1993, October 25, 1993, November 22, 1993, December 20, 1993, and March 14,
1994, issues of Digital's Customer Update.

