                   DIGITAL INTRODUCES HIGH PERFORMANCE
       ALPHA AXP RISC TECHNOLOGY TO INDUSTRY STANDARD VMEbus WITH
                    NEW SINGLE BOARD COMPUTER FAMILY

 Industry Standards: New Products Are The First PCI-based VMEbus SBC
 Family;  Products Feature Other Key Standards -- VME, SCSI, EISA.

 MAYNARD, Mass. -- March 1, 1994 --  Setting the most competitive
 price/performance ratio in the VME board industry, Digital Equipment
 Corporation today introduced a new family of VMEbus single board
 computers based on the high-performance Alpha AXP 64-bit RISC
 architecture. As the first VMEbus single board computer family to offer
 the PCI (Peripheral Component Interface) industry standard as an
 on-board interconnect, Digital's three new products, known as AXPvme
 64, AXPvme 64LC and AXPvme 160, provide a potent combination of high
 performance, competitive price/performance, industry standards and
 realtime operating system support integral to OEMs, distributors and
 end users working with realtime embedded systems. The realtime
 operating system support is provided through DEC OSF/1, a full-function
 realtime UNIX, and VxWorksfor Alpha AXP, a microkernel executive.

 "A significant number of customers in the realtime embedded market are
 moving to Alpha AXP technology," said Larry Cabrinety, Vice President
 of the Components and Peripherals Business Unit within Digital.  "They
 can look to Digital to provide total support for Alpha AXP chips and
 boards, realtime software and full worldwide service and support,
 including our contract manufacturing capabilities."

 The three single board computers (SBCs) have already been previewed
 under non-disclosure to manufacturers and distributors around the
 world. According to Digital's Single Board Computer Program Manager
 John Giudice, Raytheon has worked with Digital to produce Extended
 Environment versions of the products.

 "Working in parallel with Digital, we have developed high-performance,
 low-cost Extended Environment versions of Alpha AXPvme boards that
 offer all of the key standards and realtime software necessary to meet
 the stringent demands of our U.S. and European defense customers," said
 Jeff Hathway, a sales manager with Raytheon Company.  "The Alpha AXPvme
 line is the backbone behind our next generation product offering to
 customers with environmental requirements which exceed the capability
 of commercial counterparts."

 "Digital's new AXPvme 160 board sets a new price/performance benchmark
 for the high-end RISC VME board market," said Market Analyst Dan Baker
 of Venture Development Corp., a Natick, MA-based market research firm
 covering the VME industry. "It should be an especially strong offering
 in applications such as medical imaging, simulation and tele-
 communications."

 Baker stated, "I'm impressed by the substantial investment of
 engineering and marketing talent Digital is putting into its VME
 business.  There's no question Digital intends to be a VME player for
 the long haul. Unlike Sun Microsystems and Silicon Graphics, UNIX
 workstation vendors that have moved away from VME, Digital sees VME as
 a long-term market opportunity."

 The AXPvme line comprises three Alpha AXP single board computers:

 1. AXPvme 64: VMEbus single board computer based on the 64 MHz DECchip
    21068 CPU; standard single slot VME 6U module; with on-board
    Ethernet transceiver interface, two serial ports, 256 KB external
    cache and on-board SCSI-2 interface.

 2. AXPvme 64LC: A low-cost version of AXPvme 64, which includes
    on-board Ethernet support but does not offer external cache and on
    board SCSI-2.

 3. AXPvme 160: VMEbus single board computer based on the 160 MHz
    DECchip 21066 CPU; double-height VMEbus 6U module; with on-board
    Ethernet transceiver interface, two serial ports, 256 KB external
    cache and on-board SCSI-2 interface.

 The AXPvme 64 is a low-cost SBC specifically for competitive product
 design, while the AXPvme 160 is a high-performance SBC specifically for
 demanding, complex applications.

 The Alpha AXP 64-bit RISC microprocessors, specifically DECchip 21068
 and 21066, fuel the AXPvme line with extremely fast, competitive
 performance. Digital Applications Engineering Manager Joe Falcone
 reports performance of the

 21068 and 21066 as follows:

      21068:         30 SPECint92        40 SPECfp92

      21066:         70 SPECint92        90 SPECfp92

 Next Generation Alpha AXP Technology Supercharges SBC Performance
 Levels........

 The Alpha AXP architecture, the world's most powerful platform, can now
 be incorporated into OEM product designs quickly and cost-effectively
 via the AXPvme line. "In addition, Alpha technology provides one single
 architecture that spans from the microprocessor up to the mainframe
 level, paving the way for a scalable, durable hardware investment,"
 Falcone said.

 The DECchip 21066 and 21068 microprocessors integrate a superscalar,
 superpipelined CPU and FPU with on-chip instruction and data caches of
 8KB each.  Both chips integrate functions that are key to the embedded
 computing market: PCI controller, memory controller, and external cache
 controller.

 The AXPvme Family Provides Optimum Price/Performance Levels (Note:
 Pricing is U.S. only)........

 Single quantity prices for the AXPvme family start at $2995 for the
 AXPvme 64LC, $3495 for the AXPvme 64, and $4995 for the AXPvme 160 with
 quantity discounts available.  The AXPvme board family offers the
 industry's most competitive price/performance differential,
 outperforming well-known VME board vendors such as Motorola,
 Hewlett-Packard and Force, according to Giudice.  Comparable products
 currently available from these vendors are priced between $2995 and
 $7995. "Customers are paying 20 to 100 percent more for products that
 perform at 50 percent slower rates than the AXPvme boards," said
 Giudice.

 According to Giudice, many manufacturers require higher performance
 SBCs that are priced similarly to lower-performance SBCs currently
 available.  "We can offer these users a leap in performance at the same
 prices they were paying before for lower-performance products."

 PCI On-Board Interconnect Provides Industry-Standard Connection To The
 CPU As Well As Higher Throughput........

 The AXPvme line uses PCI as the on-board bus for high-performance
 Ethernet, SCSI-2, and VMEbus interfaces. In addition, the AXPvme line
 supports an IEEE P1386.1 PCI mezzanine option card for users to expand
 I/O functionality that may be needed for their project, such as a
 second Ethernet, graphics display, ATM or FDDI.  The PCI bus is clocked
 at 32 MHz for a peak bandwidth of 128 MB per second, according to
 Falcone.

 The proposed IEEE PCI mezzanine standard forms a part of Digital's PCI
 I/O strategy and provides a common systems architecture among Alpha AXP
 generations and system families.  This standard was jointly developed
 by Digital and other SBC manufacturers to create a common format for
 option cards across vendors.  With the PCI interconnect, CPU and
 interface chips may change among system types and generations, but the
 peripherals can remain the same.

 "The use of PCI as an on-board bus is extremely attractive to OEMs
 because it provides a high performance, industry standard connection to
 the CPU, which, in turn, delivers a much higher throughput than the VME
 standard," said Giudice.  By adding a customized PCI daughter card to
 an SBC, users can build complete systems that meet their specific
 needs, according to Giudice.

 The Alpha AXPvme Line Provides An Extensive Realtime Software
 Environment........

 The AXPvme line provides extensive software support, allowing OEMs to
 select one of two advanced realtime operating systems: VxWorks for
 Alpha AXP or DEC OSF/1 UNIX operating systems.  Both options cover a
 wide range of performance and functionality required by realtime and
 embedded applications and offer application portability, interopera-
 bility and support of key industry standards.

 VxWorks for Alpha AXP, Digital's enhanced version of the popular Wind
 River Systems solution, is a microkernel executive designed for
 embedded realtime applications. It features a priority-based, fully
 pre-emptive multi-tasking kernel and is used in those applications that
 require the highest level of realtime performance and determinism.

