 PRESS RELEASE

 European Breakthrough for Wageningen Agricultural University

 FIRST PART OF 'ELECTRONIC HIGHWAY' IMPLEMENTED IN WAGENINGEN

 Wageningen, 31 March 1994 -- Today, Wageningen Agricultural University
 was the venue for the first operational implementation in Europe of the
 Electronic Highway. In making this step, the Agricultural University
 has taken the lead in building an electronic highway network. By
 linking the University's Ethernet local area networks through the local
 cable television infrastructure, a 10 Mbit/sec electronic highway has
 evolved enabling high-speed transfer of 400 text pages per second.

 With its 2,500 staff and 7,000 students, the Agricultural University is
 housed in more than 50 buildings throughout the town of Wageningen. In
 1991, the local area networks of these buildings were connected to a 22
 kilometer fibre backbone network. This infrastructure is also used by
 the Agricultural Research Service and a number of private companies.

 The software applications which are currently provided through the
 network such as CWIS (Campus Wide Information System), GIS
 (Geographical Information System) and future multimedia applications
 are demanding ever-increasing network performance (higher bandwidths)
 to meet network user requirements both in terms of capacity and
 availability.

 Backed by a SURFnet subsidy, the University, NKM and Digital
 investigated the capacity of the cable television network to provide a
 solution for existing and future bandwidth demands. It appeared that
 this could be achieved by using Digital's ChannelWorks product to
 establish a physical connection between Ethernet LANs and the cable
 television (CATV) network. This makes the project the first-ever
 operational implementation of ChannelWorks in a European CATV network.

 Using the CATV infrastructure, low-cost data connections can be offered
 through an 'Electronic Highway' from the University's Information and
 Datacom Centre, offering a 10 Mbit/s transfer rate which equals 400 A4
 text pages per second. In addition, the University's Campus Wide
 Information System enables connections to be set up with local,
 domestic and international information suppliers.

 NKM is a Dutch cable operating company which was established in 1987 by
 the National Investment Bank and the Internationale Nederlanden Group.
 Since 1994, NKM is a fully owned subsidiary of N.V. Centrale Antenne-
 Systemen Exploitatie Maatschappij CASEMA. Some 11,000 Wageningen
 households make use of the services offered by NKM.

 SURFnet is the Dutch national network for research and higher education
 and is owned by Stichting SURF (51 percent) and PTT Telecom (49
 percent). SURFnet also initiates and manages projects for new IT
 services. These projects are jointly funded by Stichting SURF and the
 government.

 Digital Equipment B.V., a subsidiary of Digital Equipment Corporation,
 is a leading supplier in open client/server solutions ranging from
 personal computing to integrated worldwide information systems.
 Digital's product offering includes Alpha AXP platforms, storage,
 networking software and services which combined with the market-
 oriented solutions of its business partners help organisations to
 achieve a competitive edge in the dynamic environment of the modern
 international marketplace.

 Digital, the Digital logo, Alpha AXP and ChannelWorks are trademarks
 of Digital Equipment Corporation.

 SPEECH - Arie van Leusden

 Good afternoon ladies and gentlemen,

 My name is Arie van Leusden, and I am the Managing Director of Digital
 Equipment in the Netherlands.

 It is now more than ten years since Al Gore, now Vice President of the
 United States, first introduced the then elusive concept of the
 'digital superhighway'. During the decade that followed, the term
 languished in relative obscurity.

 Gates talked of experiments. Today in Wageningen, we are talking about
 the digital highway as cold fact. You will be able to assess this for
 yourself during the demonstrations later on.

 Technically speaking, I need not be physically present here at this
 moment. These days, we can communicate and make presentations using
 digital connections, but not through interlinked digital cable
 networks, such as the one put into operation today. NKM's regional
 cable television network has not yet been connected with other cable
 networks in other parts of the country. So today I sped over the
 asphalt highway to Wageningen, in the traditional manner.

 Digital enables new technologies to develop and be implemented; not
 only with today's cable television highway, but also with Client/Server
 solutions.

 This morning, I was in Eindhoven at the close of our three-day seminar
 on current implementations of Open Client/Server. While others only
 talk about client/server solutions, Digital actually introduces them
 into our working environment.

 To successfully implement such projects, we as an organisation must
 open up to the consequences of the transformation from an industrial
 economy to an information society. It's from this perspective that we
 can provide our customers with the practical help they need to meet the
 rapidly changing requirements of their clients.

 Digital therefore has to look beyond the traditional view of
 automation. This means coming up with solutions which integrate
 existing systems. It means making interconnecting existing systems, and
 combining them with new technology. The objective is always to create
 added value for our customers. We must enable them to respond in a
 flexible manner to the dynamic needs of the modern information society.

 In practice, this translates into building bridges and making
 connections, resulting in extensive, flexible networks which enable
 people and organisations to exchange information easily and at any
 time. To support this goal, Digital, together with 1,300 leading
 software suppliers, is currently offering over 14,000 practical Open
 Client/Server applications.

 It is strategically important for Digital to stand out as a supplier of
 transparent, easily expandable network solutions, which provide
 universal access to information. The fulfilment of this strategy is
 represented by the Open Client/Server which I just mentioned, as well
 as in the project we will be talking about here this afternoon.

 Last year, Digital was reorganized into strategic business units. A
 number of these, our Customer Business Units, are concentrating on
 sectors in which Digital has been successful over the years, and also
 on new growth markets. Through one of our strategic business units,
 Communication, Education and Media, Digital focusses on worldwide
 automation solutions which involve telecommunications, training, cable
 television, media and the amusement industry. Today's event proves that
 Digital is able to quickly implement this strategy in practice, now,
 while others are only talking about it.

 And we will actually need this capacity. According to Nick Negroponte
 of the Massachussets Institute of Technology, no less than 95% of all
 information will be available in digital form in 1998!

 Although our ChannelWorks products and services play an essential role
 in this process, I would like to make clear that Digital is far from a
 jack of all trades. This project was only realised thanks to intensive
 and enjoyable cooperation with the Agricultural University, cable
 operator NKM Networks and the SURF Foundation. All participants have
 recognised the importance of these new possibilities. We were able to
 carry out this innovative project together, drawing on one another's
 expertise and experience.

 Besides this pleasurable cooperation, I find the integration of
 information technology and cable connections the most intriguing
 aspect. Even more so when observing how in practice this leads to the
 merging of data, image and sound within one multimedia network.

 Just imagine the options this creates for organisations in the
 periphery of the university, where the electronic highway has already
 reached people's front door. In our next product phase, the highway
 will also become financially affordable to individuals, for example
 students. For example, through the joint use of one connection in
 student houses; say, one common sliproad onto the digital highway. The
 term 'tele-student' thereby becomes reality. Users of the digital
 highway can obtain almost instant access to an ocean of information
 through connections to regional, national and international expertise
 networks 24 hours per day.

 Ladies and gentlemen, from today, the digital highway in Europe is a
 fact. With this breakthrough project in Wageningen, Digital has once
 again proven itself as an innovator. I am convinced that this project
 is only the beginning. The digital highway as it has now been realised
 here can offer a solution for hospitals, publishers, large enterprises,
 governments, et cetera.

 This project is now being executed with our partners here in
 Wageningen. Let it be clear that we are also looking forward to
 bringing other, similar innovative projects in other environments to a
 similarly good conclusion.

 Ladies, and gentlemen, Digital is ready to take on that challenge.

 Thank you for your attention.



